// Original practice passages. These are fictional news-style exercises, not reports of real events.
const banks={
B1:[
['A Library on Wheels','流动图书馆','A small town has a new library on wheels. The bright blue bus stops near schools and parks. Children can take a book home and bring it back the next week. Volunteers also read stories to younger visitors. The team hopes more families will enjoy reading together.'],
['A Greener School','学校里的绿色计划','Students at a local school are growing vegetables in a small garden. They water the plants before class and learn where food comes from. At the end of each month, they share the vegetables with families in the neighborhood. Teachers say the garden makes learning more fun.'],
['A Park for Everyone','人人都能享受的公园','A city has opened a new park beside the river. It has wide paths, shady trees, and a play area for children. There are also places for older people to sit and talk. Local residents helped choose the design at a community meeting.'],
['The Repair Café','修理咖啡馆','A new café is helping people fix broken things. Visitors can bring a lamp, a chair, or a small radio. Volunteers show them how to make simple repairs while they enjoy a cup of tea. The project saves money and keeps useful items out of the trash.'],
['A Special Delivery','特别的送货服务','A grocery store has started delivering food to older neighbors. Drivers take orders by phone and bring bags to the front door. The service is especially helpful for people who cannot easily leave home. Customers say a friendly conversation is sometimes as welcome as the food.'],
['Walking to School','步行上学计划','Parents and children are walking to school together in one neighborhood. They meet at a street corner every morning and follow the same safe route. The group helps children get exercise and gives parents time to talk. Organizers hope even more families will join next month.'],
['A Small Market','小型社区集市','Every Saturday, local farmers sell fresh food in the town square. Shoppers can buy apples, bread, eggs, and flowers. Many people enjoy talking directly to the people who grow their food. The market also gives small businesses a chance to meet new customers.'],
['A Beach Cleanup','海滩清洁行动','Dozens of volunteers met on a beach early Sunday morning. They picked up bottles, bags, and other waste along the water. By noon, the group had filled many large bags. Organizers reminded visitors to take their trash home and protect the sea.'],
['Music After Work','下班后的音乐','A group of neighbors has started a free music club. Members meet after work to sing and play instruments together. Some are experienced musicians, while others are trying music for the first time. The club plans to perform in the park when the weather gets warmer.'],
['A Home for Animals','动物的新家','An animal shelter is asking local families to help care for dogs and cats. The shelter provides food and advice while volunteers offer a safe temporary home. Staff say even a short stay with a family can help an animal feel more comfortable around people.'] ],
B2:[
['The Value of a Shorter Workweek','一周工作四天的价值','A group of small businesses is testing a shorter workweek without cutting pay. Managers hope that fewer meetings and clearer priorities will help employees finish their work on time. Workers say they have more energy for family and rest. The companies will compare customer satisfaction and staff wellbeing before deciding whether to continue.'],
['Rethinking Food Waste','重新思考食物浪费','Restaurants in one district are experimenting with smaller portions and clearer menus to reduce food waste. Customers can order more if they are still hungry, while kitchens track what remains at the end of each day. Owners say the changes save money, although predicting demand remains a challenge.'],
['A Second Life for Clothes','旧衣服的第二次生命','A growing number of shoppers are choosing repaired and secondhand clothing. One local workshop teaches people to mend a torn sleeve or replace a zipper. Supporters say keeping clothes in use longer can reduce waste. However, they note that transport and cleaning also have environmental costs.'],
['Learning Across Generations','跨代学习','A community center has brought teenagers and older residents together for weekly technology lessons. Young volunteers explain how to use online services, while older participants share stories and practical skills. Organizers say the exchange works best when everyone has something to teach and something to learn.'],
['The Future of City Trees','城市树木的未来','City planners are studying where more trees could reduce summer heat. Trees can provide shade and make streets more pleasant, but they also need water and long-term care. Residents have been invited to identify hot areas and help decide which species are suitable for each neighborhood.'],
['Better Bus Information','更清晰的公交信息','A transit agency is testing signs that show when the next bus is expected to arrive. Passengers say the information makes waiting less stressful, especially when a service is delayed. The agency will check whether the predictions remain accurate during bad weather and busy periods.'],
['The Cost of Fast Delivery','快速配送的代价','Online shoppers often expect packages to arrive the next day. Yet faster delivery can require more vehicles and less efficient routes. A delivery company is testing a slower option that combines orders going to the same area. Customers can choose the service that best fits their needs.'],
['Local News Finds New Readers','地方新闻的新读者','A small newspaper is inviting readers to suggest questions for its reporters. Editors say the idea helps them understand which local issues deserve attention. They still check every claim before publication and explain how they reached their conclusions. The project has also encouraged more residents to attend public meetings.'],
['A New Way to Share Tools','共享工具的新方式','Instead of buying tools they may use only once, residents can borrow them from a neighborhood workshop. Members pay a small fee and receive safety instructions before taking equipment home. The service can make repairs more affordable, although volunteers must regularly maintain and replace worn tools.'],
['Protecting Sleep','保护睡眠','A workplace wellbeing program is encouraging employees to think about their sleep habits. Sessions cover regular bedtimes, evening light, and the effects of caffeine. Organizers emphasize that no single routine works for everyone. They also remind participants to seek professional advice when sleep problems persist.'] ],
C1:[
['Who Benefits from Urban Renewal?','城市更新让谁受益？','A proposed redevelopment promises new housing, public space, and better transport links. Yet residents are asking whether the people who already live nearby will be able to afford the resulting neighborhood. Planners have agreed to publish a fuller assessment and consult tenants as well as property owners. The debate illustrates how the benefits and costs of renewal may be distributed unevenly.'],
['The Limits of Recycling','回收的边界','Recycling campaigns can make waste more visible, but they do not eliminate the resources used to produce disposable goods. Researchers argue that reuse and longer product lifetimes should be considered alongside collection rates. Measuring success solely by the volume recycled, they warn, may obscure opportunities to prevent waste in the first place.'],
['When Algorithms Guide Hiring','算法参与招聘时','Some employers use automated tools to sort job applications, hoping to handle large numbers more consistently. Critics question whether the systems reproduce patterns embedded in historical hiring data. Employers testing such tools are being urged to examine outcomes, document decisions, and provide applicants with a meaningful way to challenge mistakes.'],
['Measuring a City’s Wellbeing','如何衡量城市福祉','Economic growth can reveal important changes in a city, but it says relatively little about how residents experience daily life. A new dashboard combines income data with access to housing, transport, and green space. Its designers caution that averages can conceal substantial differences between neighborhoods and demographic groups.'],
['The Promise of Citizen Science','公民科学的潜力','Volunteers are helping researchers document seasonal changes in local wildlife. Their observations can cover a wider area than a small professional team could manage alone. Nevertheless, uneven participation may distort the results. Scientists are therefore designing training and checks to improve the quality and interpretation of the data.'],
['The Hidden Price of Convenience','便利背后的隐性成本','Digital services can save consumers time, yet the apparent convenience may depend on poorly understood labor and infrastructure. A new discussion series asks users to consider who maintains the systems they rely on. The organizers hope that making these dependencies visible will lead to better informed choices and public debate.'],
['A Different Approach to Flooding','应对洪水的新思路','Rather than relying exclusively on barriers, one region is exploring ways to restore wetlands and give rivers more room. Proponents say such measures can reduce flood risk while supporting wildlife. Landowners, however, want clearer evidence about maintenance costs and how responsibility would be shared over time.'],
['Trust in Public Information','公众信息与信任','A health communication team is testing whether acknowledging uncertainty improves public understanding. Its approach explains what is known, what remains unclear, and when advice may change. The team recognizes that transparency alone cannot guarantee trust; people also need evidence that institutions respond consistently to their concerns.'],
['The Case for Repairable Devices','让电子设备更易维修','Advocates for repairable electronics argue that access to parts, manuals, and affordable service can extend the life of devices. Manufacturers point to concerns about safety and protecting sensitive data. Policymakers face the task of balancing those concerns against the potential economic and environmental benefits of repair.'],
['Preserving Language in a Digital Age','数字时代的语言保存','A group of speakers is building a digital archive of stories, songs, and everyday conversation in a threatened language. The project is guided by community members, who decide what can be shared publicly. Organizers stress that recording a language is valuable, but keeping it in daily use requires sustained opportunities to speak it.'] ],
C2:[
['The Paradox of Efficiency','效率的悖论','Efforts to make a system more efficient can inadvertently leave it less resilient when conditions change. A tightly optimized supply chain may reduce costs in ordinary circumstances while retaining little capacity to absorb disruption. The relevant question is therefore not whether efficiency is desirable, but how much flexibility a society is prepared to maintain in exchange for security.'],
['Whose Evidence Counts?','谁的证据被看见？','Public decisions frequently rely on evidence that appears neutral because it has been translated into numbers. Yet the choice of what to measure, whose experiences to include, and which uncertainties to disclose is itself consequential. A more accountable process would scrutinize those choices without dismissing quantitative analysis as inherently suspect.'],
['The Politics of Waiting','等待背后的公共政策','Waiting is often treated as an individual inconvenience, though delays can also reveal how institutions distribute scarce attention. A service that is nominally available to all may prove inaccessible to people unable to take time off work. Examining waiting times alongside eligibility can therefore expose inequalities that formal rules alone leave hidden.'],
['Can a City Be Too Resilient?','城市韧性的复杂含义','Resilience has become an attractive goal for cities facing repeated shocks. The term can, however, obscure a difficult distinction between helping communities recover and expecting them to endure preventable harm. An adequate policy must ask not only how quickly a city returns to normal, but whether that normal was acceptable in the first place.'],
['The Uncertainty of Prediction','预测的不确定性','Forecasts can inform decisions without offering certainty about individual outcomes. Trouble arises when a precise numerical estimate is mistaken for a complete account of the world it describes. Responsible use requires attention to assumptions, alternative scenarios, and the possibility that the conditions underlying a model may themselves change.'],
['A Commons in the Cloud','云端的公共资源','Shared digital resources can lower barriers to knowledge, but their maintenance is rarely automatic. Contributors must decide how to fund infrastructure, resolve disputes, and prevent a small group from bearing disproportionate costs. Treating access as a public good thus raises questions about governance as well as technology.'],
['The Ambiguity of Progress','进步的多重含义','A policy may produce measurable gains while leaving some groups worse off. Describing the outcome simply as progress risks concealing both the distribution of benefits and the choices made along the way. A richer assessment would identify the trade-offs explicitly and consider whether those affected had a voice in defining success.'],
['Memory and the Public Record','记忆与公共记录','Archives are often imagined as passive repositories of the past, yet every collection reflects decisions about what was preserved and what was overlooked. Digitization can widen access while reproducing earlier omissions. Curators are consequently reconsidering not only how records are displayed, but who has the authority to describe them.'],
['The Value of Unproductive Time','无产出时间的价值','Time that yields no immediate, measurable output can nevertheless sustain creativity, care, and reflection. The difficulty is that institutions accustomed to short reporting cycles may struggle to recognize such benefits. Defending unstructured time requires more than celebrating leisure; it calls for examining the incentives that make constant activity appear indispensable.'],
['Governing What We Cannot See','治理不可见的影响','Many environmental harms accumulate gradually and remain difficult to attribute to a single source. This creates a temptation to postpone action until every causal pathway is settled. Yet delay is itself a decision, with consequences that may be unevenly distributed. Sound policy must weigh uncertainty against the costs of both acting and waiting.'] ]};
const tips=[{phrase:'want to',ipa:'/wɑːnə/',title:'弱读与连接',explain:'自然语流中 want to 常接近 wanna；正式、慢速朗读时也可以清楚读出两个词。'}, {phrase:'pick it up',ipa:'/pɪk ɪɾ ʌp/',title:'辅音连元音 + 闪音',explain:'pick 的 /k/ 连到 it；美音里元音之间的 /t/ 常变成轻弹的 /ɾ/，听起来像轻轻的 d。'}, {phrase:'take a',ipa:'/teɪk ə/',title:'辅音连元音',explain:'take 结尾的 /k/ 顺势连到 a 的弱读 /ə/，不要在两个词中间停顿。'}, {phrase:'and bring',ipa:'/ən brɪŋ/',title:'弱读',explain:'非重读的 and 常弱读成 /ən/，但具体发音会随着语速和说话人变化。'}, {phrase:'next day',ipa:'/nek(s) deɪ/',title:'辅音简化',explain:'连续辅音较多时，next 的 /t/ 可能不完全释放；保留节奏，别把 t 硬加一个元音。'}, {phrase:'tell him',ipa:'/tel ɪm/',title:'h 弱化',explain:'非重读的 him 在自然语流中可能弱化 /h/，前一个词尾音顺势连上；并不是所有情况都要省略 h。'}, {phrase:'at the',ipa:'/æt ðə/',title:'失去爆破',explain:'at 末尾的 /t/ 在后续辅音前可能不完全爆破，舌位到位后直接过渡到 /ð/。'}];
